#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158539 :tutorial/content "The **variance** of a continuous random variable is the expected squared difference between the variable and its expected value.\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the variance is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{S}(x-\\text{E}[X])^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn practice, though, it's easier to use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the continuous random variable $X$ with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}, \\quad 1 < x < 3\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{(\\frac{13}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "24436a92-f030-567a-aa1c-938371716e3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4805 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
